  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.2. Increased capacity of member States to ensure safe and healthy working conditions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Occupational Health Advisory Committee (OHAC) has developed the National Occupational Health Services Strategic Plan 2020-2024, which addresses specific health risks in workplaces due to exposure to hazards such as chemicals and asbestos as well as COVID-19 virus. The Strategic Plan was endorsed by the National Occupational Health and Safety Advisory Board on 16 December 2020, and the Ministry of Employment, Productivity and Industrial Relations had started implementing the Strategic Plan through National Occupational Health Service. The ILO has participated as an active member of the Occupational Health Advisory Committee (OHAC), together with WHO, since the formation of the OHAC as a sub-committee of the National OHS Advisory Board (NOHSAB) in 2017. In this Committee, the ILO has given technical advice and financial support to Goal 1 on the Legal and Institutional Framework: Well Being of Workers improved through Structured Legal OH Framework in Fiji by encouraging ratification of relevant Conventions &#x2013; C161, C162, C170 and C187; and Goal 3 &#x2013; Strengthening Occupational Health Information Systems through technical and financial support funding a paperless inspection system for labour administration services and OHS services.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">7.2.1. Number of member States with national OSH policies or programmes, accompanied by institutional frameworks, addressing specific risks</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
